1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,634
Can an AI detect your true emotion just from the tone of your voice? Most

2
00:00:05,634 --> 00:00:11,269
speech models cheat by memorizing speaker voices. Here is how we solved it with PyTorch.

3
00:00:11,269 --> 00:00:17,035
If you split speech data at random, your accuracy is fiction. We enforced

4
00:00:17,035 --> 00:00:22,799
a strict speaker-independent split on twenty-four actors, testing only on completely unseen voices.

5
00:00:22,799 --> 00:00:28,904
We engineered a 280-dimensional acoustic feature vector capturing 40 MFCC bands, pitch

6
00:00:28,904 --> 00:00:35,009
velocity, and spectral contrast, trained with a deep 1D Convolutional Neural Network.

7
00:00:35,009 --> 00:00:40,140
Here is the live project! Featuring live three-second microphone recording

8
00:00:40,140 --> 00:00:45,269
with animated waveforms, instant emotion prediction, and a recordings history deck.

9
00:00:45,269 --> 00:00:50,254
We delivered honest benchmark metrics. High arousal overlaps between anger

10
00:00:50,254 --> 00:00:55,239
and joy, while subtle sadness shares acoustic prosody with calm speech.

11
00:00:55,239 --> 00:01:00,764
Thank you for watching! The project is open source on GitHub and live

12
00:01:00,764 --> 00:01:06,289
on GitHub Pages. Star the repository, test your voice, and let's connect on LinkedIn!

